               <msContents>
                  <summary>Siddur for the entire year, Sephardi rite</summary>
                  <msItem n="1">
                     <title key="work_282">Siddur for the entire year according to the Sephardi
                        rite</title>
                     <title xml:lang="he" key="work_282">סדור מנהג ספרד לכל השנה</title>
                     <note>Calendar for the years <date calendar="Julian">1485-1556</date> on f.
                        209r.</note>
                     <note>The instructions are partly in Judeo-Portuguese.</note>
                     <note><locus>Ff. 49-56, 142-143, 160-161</locus> are later replacements of
                        missing leaves.</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="he">Hebrew</textLang>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <physDesc>
                  <objectDesc form="codex">
                     <supportDesc material="perg">
                        <support>parchment</support>
                        <extent>ff. 318 <dimensions type="leaf" unit="mm">
                              <height>83</height>
                              <width>65</width>
                           </dimensions>
                        </extent>
                        <foliation>Foliation in Arabic numerals.</foliation>
                     </supportDesc>
                  </objectDesc>
                  <handDesc>
                     <summary>Sephardi semi-cursive script</summary>
                     <handNote>The initial words are in Sephardi square script; some of the refrains
                        and liturgical instructions are in Sephardic cursive script.</handNote>
                  </handDesc>
                  <decoDesc>
                     <p>Four decorated initial word pages (<locus>ff. 2r, 15r, 39v, 77r</locus>)
                        with penwork flourishing in lilac within the initial word panel, and
                        coloured penwork foliage scrolls occupying the margins. The initial words
                        themselves are gilt.</p>
                     <p>Monochrome penwork flourishing in lilac (with one exception, <locus>f.
                           25r</locus>, where the penwork is in brown ink) around initial words on
                           <locus>ff. 25r, 151v, 213r, 221v, 247r, 276r, 284r, 297v</locus>.</p>
                  </decoDesc>
                  <bindingDesc>
                     <p>Fine-grained dark brown leather on thin wooden boards, fastened with two
                        iron clasps. All edges are gilt. A black leather label bearing the current
                        shelfmark ('CAN. OR. 108.') at the base of the spine.</p>
                     <condition>There are no pastedowns. Some erosions from the iron nails of the
                        clasps on the paper flyleaves. One of the clasps has partially split in
                        two.</condition>
                  </bindingDesc>
               </physDesc>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origPlace>[Lisbon?]</origPlace>
                     <origDate calendar="Julian" notBefore="1484" notAfter="1485">ca.
                        1484/1485</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance>On <locus>???</locus>, an ownership inscription by <persName>Ḥananel
                        ha-Levi ben <foreign xml:lang="he">חננעל הלוי בן</foreign> [...] </persName>
                     (remainder of name illegible).</provenance>
                  <acquisition><persName key="person_2384880">Matteo Luigi Canonici</persName>'s
                     (1727-1805) collections passed to his brother Giuseppe, and on his death in
                     1807 to <persName key="person_1">Giovanni Perissinotti</persName> and <persName
                        key="person_2">Girolamo Cardina</persName>, who divided them. To the former
                     fell the MSS., then about 3550 in number, and, after many attempts to sell
                     them, the Bodleian became the purchaser of the greater part in <date
                        calendar="Gregorian">1817</date>, for £5444 5s. 1d., or including incidental
                     expenses about £6030, the largest single purchase ever made by the Library. The
                     formal list of volumes handed over was signed on May 18, 1817, and the books
                     probably arrived later that year.</acquisition>
               </history>
